# Learn more https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/getting-started-with-git/ignoring-files

# dependencies
node_modules/

# Expo
.expo/
dist/
web-build/
# expo-env.d.ts is TRACKED, despite the line below and the note inside the
# file itself. It is generated, but it is also the only thing declaring
# expo's ambient module types — CSS imports especially. Untracked,
# `tsc --noEmit` passes on any machine that has run the dev server and fails
# on a fresh clone, which is exactly what CI is. Tracking it makes the
# typecheck reproducible; it regenerates to identical content, so it
# doesn't churn.
#
# The pattern is listed anyway, and listing it costs nothing: **.gitignore
# has no effect on a file git already tracks.** What it does buy is silence
# from expo-cli. Every `expo lint` and `expo start` runs
# `upsertGitIgnoreContents(".gitignore", "expo-env.d.ts")`, which returns
# early and writes nothing if the file already contains a line matching
# /^expo-env.d.ts$/ — and otherwise appends a `# @generated expo-cli` block
# to the bottom of this file. Without the line below, every lint run dirties
# .gitignore with a block that contradicts this comment, and someone has to
# notice and strip it before committing. Twice in one session, in practice.
#
# So: the line satisfies expo-cli, and the file stays tracked. To prove that
# to yourself, `git ls-files expo-env.d.ts` still lists it and editing it
# still shows up in `git status`. Note plain
# `git check-ignore -v expo-env.d.ts` exits 1 and prints nothing here — not
# because the rule is missing, but because check-ignore skips tracked files.
# `git check-ignore -v --no-index expo-env.d.ts` is the one that names it,
# and that difference is the whole point restated: tracked beats ignored.
expo-env.d.ts

# Native
.kotlin/
*.orig.*
*.jks
*.p8
*.p12
*.key
*.mobileprovision

# Metro
.metro-health-check*

# debug
npm-debug.*
yarn-debug.*
yarn-error.*

# macOS
.DS_Store
*.pem

# Local env overrides. Note that `.env` itself is deliberately NOT ignored:
# it carries the public verdicts URL and is meant to be shared. Anything
# that shouldn't be committed belongs in `.env.local`, which Expo loads
# ahead of `.env` — see the header comment in `.env` for the full rule.
.env*.local

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo

example

# generated native folders
/ios/*
!/ios/ci_scripts/
/android

# local agent settings
.claude/settings.local.json

# Screenshots pulled down by scripts/testflight-feedback.mjs --download.
# Tester-submitted images of their own screen, fetched to be looked at once
# and not part of the app — unlike docs/evidence/, there's nothing here worth
# keeping a record of.
/.testflight-feedback/

# Stray feed-checker output at the repo root. Reports under docs/evidence/
# are deliberately tracked — they're the record of when each source was last
# verified alive. Root-only pattern so scripts/check-feeds.sh output lands in
# version control where it's useful.
/feed-status*.txt

# Wrangler's local dev state (the .wrangler/ cache and miniflare's simulated
# KV), created by the `worker` entry in .claude/launch.json. Build output, not
# config — wrangler.toml is the tracked part.
worker/.wrangler/

# Worker logs pulled down by scripts/worker-logs.mjs. Cloudflare keeps these
# 3 days and this is a local copy of a window someone looked at — same call as
# .testflight-feedback/ above: read once, acted on in a commit, not a record.
/.worker-logs/
